<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.6.1: Using Linear Functions to Describe Constant Speed</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01830</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.6.1: Using Linear Functions to Describe Constant Speed</md:title>
    <md:uuid>dd80717c-4d2d-4b7d-a7d0-8ec2209f8e75</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>A cannon is 10 feet off the ground. It launches a cannonball straight up with a velocity of 406 feet per second.</para>
<para>Imagine that there is no gravity and that the cannonball continues to travel upward with the same velocity.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
  <item>Answer questions a–f using the table with the heights of the cannonball at different times.</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Seconds
     </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Distance above ground (feet)
     </emphasis></entry></row>
  <row>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        10
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        a. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        b. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        c. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        d. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        e. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(t\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        f. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise id="_3a577da6-6105-4e46-85c7-cb4e068e4bfe" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> How far above the ground is the cannonball at 1 second?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! The cannonball is 416 feet above the ground at 1 second.</para><para>The correct answer is: The cannonball is 416 feet above the ground at 1 second.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_6bb155ed-6a1c-4114-9f67-9ad53bae0313" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> How far above the ground is the cannonball at 2 seconds?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! The cannonball is 822 feet above the ground at 2 seconds.</para><para>The correct answer is: The cannonball is 822 feet above the ground at 2 seconds.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_391c299f-b35d-4c3f-b372-744ba36d15f2" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> How far above the ground is the cannonball at 3 seconds?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! The cannonball is 1,228 feet above the ground at 3 seconds.</para><para>The correct answer is: The cannonball is 1228 feet above the ground at 3 seconds.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_74878a62-1f11-4e7f-bf75-6d943723008b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> How far above the ground is the cannonball at 4 seconds?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! The cannonball is 1634 feet above the ground at 4 seconds.</para><para>The correct answer is: The cannonball is 1634 feet above the ground at 4 seconds.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_7676f221-e8dc-4062-b482-97ca000c4044" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> How far above the ground is the cannonball at 5 seconds?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! The cannonball is 2040 feet above the ground at 5 seconds.</para><para>The correct answer is: The cannonball is 2040 feet above the ground at 5 seconds.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How far above the ground is the cannonball at \(t\) seconds?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>The cannonball is \(10+406t\) feet above the ground at \(t\) seconds.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation to model the distance in feet, \(d\), of the ball \(t\) seconds after it was fired from the cannon, if there was no gravity.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(d=10+406t\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
